course: english
lesson: 25
title: Reading poems: meaning, sound and pictures
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A poem packs a lot into a few words, and you read it with your ears and your eyes. Even «Twinkle, twinkle, little star, / How I wonder what you are», written by Jane Taylor over two hundred years ago, has a real question in it: the speaker is looking up and wondering what a star really is. The speaker is the voice in the poem.
explain: Poems paint pictures with words, called imagery. William Blake wrote: «Tyger Tyger, burning bright, / In the forests of the night». The tiger is not on fire: its orange stripes seem to glow like flames in the dark forest, so it looks powerful and dangerous. William Wordsworth wrote «I wandered lonely as a cloud»: a simile, comparing himself to a cloud with «as», drifting along all alone.
explain: Sound matters too. Robert Louis Stevenson wrote about a train: «Faster than fairies, faster than witches, / Bridges and houses, hedges and ditches». Read it aloud and the quick beat sounds like wheels rushing on rails. Lots of «s» sounds, as in «the snake slid softly through the silent grass» (our own line), can sound soft, sly or creepy. Rhymes depend on accent: in Yorkshire, «cut» and «put» rhyme, but in London they don't.
explain: Some poems ask big questions. Christina Rossetti wrote: «Who has seen the wind? / Neither I nor you». We never see the wind itself, only what it does to the leaves. Some poems are nonsense on purpose: Edward Lear's «The Owl and the Pussy-cat went to sea / In a beautiful pea-green boat» isn't realistic at all, it's fun. And lots of poems don't rhyme; that is called free verse. Chloe in Cardiff says, «This poem's lush, but I don't get it» (lush is Welsh slang for lovely): liking a poem before you understand it is normal, so read it again.
fact: The speaker is the voice that talks in a poem.
fact: Imagery means pictures made with words.
fact: A simile compares two things using «like» or «as»: lonely as a cloud.
fact: Rhythm is the beat of a poem, and it can match the topic, like a fast beat for a rushing train.
fact: Repeating «s» sounds is called sibilance; it can sound soft, like wind, or sly, like a snake.
fact: The mood of a poem is the feeling it gives, such as happy, sad, scary or calm.
fact: Free verse is poetry without regular rhyme; poems use line breaks, imagery and rhythm, not only rhyme.
fact: Nonsense verse is silly on purpose, written for fun and for the sound of the words.
fact: Rhymes can change with accent: in many northern English accents «cut» and «put» rhyme.
fact: Rossetti's poem «Who has seen the wind?» says we know the wind is there by what it does.
fact: The poems by Jane Taylor, William Blake, William Wordsworth, Christina Rossetti, Robert Louis Stevenson and Edward Lear are old and free for anyone to share.
fact: A stanza is a group of lines in a poem, like a paragraph in a story.
fact: Slang: «lush» (Wales) means lovely; «mid» means average.
exercise: «Twinkle, twinkle, little star, / How I wonder what you are.» What is the speaker wondering? | What the star really is | Why it is so cold and dark outside at night | Where the Moon has gone tonight | When it will be time for bed
exercise: Why does Blake say the tiger is «burning bright»? | Its stripes seem to glow like fire in the dark | The forest is really on fire all around it | The tiger is very hot | The tiger is holding a burning torch to light its way through the forest
exercise: «I wandered lonely as a cloud.» What is compared? | The poet and a cloud | A cloud and a big grey storm moving over the hills | The sky and the sea | Two clouds
exercise: Liam reads a poem in his Yorkshire accent and the rhymes change. Why? | Rhymes depend on how words are said in each accent | He is reading the poem the wrong way and should change his accent to a posh one | The poem is broken | Yorkshire has no rhymes
exercise: Rossetti asks «Who has seen the wind?» What does the poem suggest? | We see what the wind does, not the wind itself | Some lucky people have seen the wind with their own eyes on stormy days | Wind is green | It is never windy
exercise: Chloe says the poem is «lush, but I don't get it». What is good advice? | Read it again and talk about a line you like | Give up, poems are only for clever people who already understand everything | Skip the hard words | Only read poems that rhyme
exercise: Stevenson's «Faster than fairies, faster than witches» is about what? | A fast train journey | A race between fairies and witches across the sky at night | A slow walk | A witch's house
exercise: A poem uses lots of «s» sounds. Why might the poet do this? | To create a soft or sly sound | Because «s» is the easiest letter to spell, so it saves the poet a lot of time | To make it rhyme | By accident
exercise: «What is the mood of the poem?» What does mood mean? | The feeling the poem gives | The number of lines | The name of the poet who wrote it a long time ago | The rhyme
exercise: Tom says, «Poems are mid, they don't even rhyme sometimes.» What is true? | Many poems don't rhyme and are still poems | A poem without rhymes is just a story that someone forgot to finish | All poems rhyme | Rhyme is the only thing that makes a poem
exercise: The Owl and the Pussy-cat go to sea in a pea-green boat. Is it realistic? | No, it's nonsense verse, written for fun | Yes, owls and cats often go sailing together in small boats | Yes, it's a true story | No, it's a news report
exercise: What is imagery? | Pictures made with words | Real photographs printed next to a poem in a book | The title at the top of the poem | The rhyme pattern of the lines
exercise: What is a stanza? | A group of lines in a poem | One word | The poet's name | The last rhyme
exercise: Which line is a simile? | Lonely as a cloud | Tyger Tyger, burning bright | Who has seen the wind? | The Owl and the Pussy-cat went to sea
test: «the tiger's literally on fire, that's why it's burning bright». What's the best reply? | It's imagery: the stripes look like flames | Yes, it's a real fire in the forest | Tigers glow in the dark like a torch | It means the tiger is angry and its fur has turned red with rage
test: ★★ In «Faster than fairies, faster than witches, / Bridges and houses, hedges and ditches», what do the bridges, houses and hedges show? | Things flashing past the train window | Places the fairies live | A shopping list | Things the witches built
test: ★★ Which is a simile? | Her smile was as bright as the sun | Her smile was the sun | She smiled | The sun smiled down at her and waved hello
test: ★★★ «Who has seen the wind? Neither I nor you.» Which other thing do we know about only by what it does, like the wind? | Gravity, which makes things fall | A tree, which we can see and touch | A cat, which we can stroke and hear | The Moon, which we can see at night
test: ★★★ In which pair would the words rhyme for a speaker from Yorkshire but not for a speaker from London? | Cut and put | Cat and hat | Star and are | Night and bright
test: ★★★ «Twinkle, twinkle, little star, / How I wonder what you are.» Which two words rhyme in a standard reading, and why do they match? | Star and are, both end with the same «ar» sound | Twinkle and little, both end with «le» | Wonder and what, both start with «w» | How and you, both are short
test: In a cartoon, a poet only writes «moon, June, spoon, tune». What does real poetry also use? | Imagery, rhythm and feeling, not only rhyme | Only rhymes that end in «oon» | Long lists of numbers | Nothing else at all, because rhyme is the whole of poetry
test: «why's the owl in a boat with a cat, that's not even possible». What kind of poem is it? | Nonsense verse, silly on purpose | A science report | A true diary | A newspaper article
test: What would happen to «Faster than fairies» if you read it very slowly, like a lullaby? | You'd lose the rushing train feeling | It would sound even faster and more exciting | It would become a different poem with new words | Nothing would change, it is the same poem
test: A poem has a slow beat and words like grey, empty and alone. What is its mood? | Sad and lonely | Happy and excited | Scary and loud | Funny and silly
test: «I wandered lonely as a cloud» — Leo says the poem is about clouds. What's closer? | It's about the poet, who felt alone like a drifting cloud | It's a weather report for the hills | It's about rain falling on the fields | It's about a lonely cloud that wandered off over the hills and got lost
exam: Identify the device employed in the line «I wandered lonely as a cloud». | Simile | Onomatopoeia | Rhetorical question | Headline
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the image of a tiger «burning bright»? | The animal's colouring is likened to flame, conveying power and danger | The animal is literally ablaze | The poet is describing a forest fire | The image is without meaning
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning poetry without regular rhyme. | Such poetry, termed free verse, is nonetheless poetry | Such writing is not poetry | Such writing is prohibited in schools by order of the examination board | Such poetry is invariably composed by children
exam: Ascertain the principal idea conveyed by the lines «Who has seen the wind? / Neither I nor you». | That the wind is perceived through its effects rather than seen directly | That the wind is visible only to poets | That the wind does not exist | That the poet is blind and therefore unable to perceive the weather
